What SPI modes really mean ?

	SPI modes are decided by 2 bits : 

		CPOL -> clock polarity (idle level of clock)
		CPHA -> clock phase (when data is sampled)

		
	Rule 1 - CPOL (Idle clock level)

		CPOL = 0 -> Clock idles Low 
		CPOL = 1 -> Clock idles High

		So just look at the clock when nothing is happening 

	Rule 2 - CPHA (which edges read data)

		CPHA = 0 -> sample on first edge 
		CPHA = 1 -> sample on second edge 

		First edge = first transition after CS goes low 

	All 4 SPI modes 

		Mode | CPOL | CPHA | Clock Idle | Data Sample
		---------------------------------------------
		 0   |  0   |  0   | LOW        | Rising edge
		 1   |  0   |  1   | LOW        | Falling edge
		 2   |  1   |  0   | HIGH       | Falling edge
		 3   |  1   |  1   | HIGH       | Rising edge


SPI Modes
---------

Mode 0 :

        CPOL : 0
        CPHA : 0

        * Master and slave will read/sample data on the rising edge
        * Data transmission takes place in falling edge
        * Master can send 1 bit info using 1 clock pulse
        * In the SPI, a full duplex communication occurs in every clock pulse
	* When the clock line is 0 slave will not read data from the MOSI pin
	* If master changing the clock voltage from 0 to +3.3V, a rising edge is detected and slave will read 1 bit information from MOSI pin and, slave will write 1 bit information on the MISO pin and master will read from the MISO pin.

	      _____
	     /		
	____/ 			slave will sample 1 bit data from MOSI 
				Master will sample 1 bit data frame MISO 

	* When falling edge is detected slave will understand now master will send the next data bit 
	* Only in the rising edge data will be transmitted

	

























































